  "query_preview": "..Main forecast concern over the next several days\nwill be the advection of low and mid level moisture from the \neast that may lead to an increase in showers and thunderstorms \nmainly east of Tucson. Morning PWAT imagery showed values up to\n3/4 of an inch across the NM counties Luna, Sierra and Dona Ana\nor NW of El Paso with this moisture gradually pushing to the\nW/NW today. There is a slight chance for mainly dry thunderstorm\nover northern Greenlee county this afternoon. Otherwise the \nscattered thunderstorms that develop today across SW NM will \nsend low-level moisture into SE Arizona with dewpoints shooting\nup into the 40s overnight across Greenlee county and eastern \nhalf of Graham and Cochise counties. Biggest forecast quandary \nfor Wednesday is how far west will the chance of showers and \nthunderstorms be. NBM keeps storms mostly across Greenlee, \nGraham and Cochise counties while some CAMS have storms as far \nwest as eastern Pinal, Pima and Santa Cruz counties. As the \nmoment sided with the NBM but won't be surprised today if NBM  \nPoPs increase as far west as Tucson on Wednesday. Anyway the \nstorms on Wednesday will be a mix of dry and wet with main \nthreat being gusty outflow winds and blowing dust east of \nTucson. 02/00z HREF neighborhood probabilities show 50%-70% \nchance of winds greater than 35 mph east of Tucson. Forecast \nconfidence for Wednesday is moderate.\n\nLingering moisture on Thursday for slight chance of storms east\nof Tucson, then moisture moves out of the area on Friday.\n\nHigh temperatures for the 7 days will run + or - a couple \ndegrees around normal.\n\n8-14 day outlooks (June 9-15)\nTemperature: Likely above normal (58%-63% chance) with the \nfirst real HOT temperatures of the year possible from June 11th \nto 14th. Low end, less than 10% chance, that Tucson and the \nlower deserts could see 110+ by the end of next week. Stay \ntuned.\n\nPrecipitation: Near normal for the majority of SE AZ. The \nexception is across SE Cochise county where that area is \nleaning slightly above normal (33%-34% chance).",
